ponent). worker/index.ts is the Worker entry: routes /_vinext/image to image optimizer, everything else to the vinext app-router handler. app/layout.tsx metadata + fonts. app/globals.css all styling. db/schema.ts + db/index.ts is D1/Drizzle scaffolding but intentionally empty (schema is a placeholder; .openai/hosting.json has d1: null so no DB bound).\n  2. Benchmark harness: benchmark/STUDY.md frozen research contract; benchmark/tasks/T1-repo-map/ (task.json contract, prompt.md, rubric.json); benchmark/schemas/ JSON schemas for task/run/result; benchmark/runs/ recorded artifacts; scripts/benchmark-*.mjs.\n- build tooling: vite.config.ts uses vinext + Cloudflare plugin + custom sites plugin that copies hosting.json and drizzle migrations into dist/.next.config.ts is minimal.\n- db: drizzle-kit configured for sqlite/D1 but schema empty.\n\n## Main request flow\n1. Browser GET / to Cloudflare Worker (worker/index.ts:29).\n2. Non-image path → vinext app-router entry (worker/index.ts:43) which runs the Next.js server rendering.\n3. layout.tsx wraps, page.tsx (client component, \"use client\") renders the study plan.\n4. On mount, page.tsx useEffect reads localStorage key \"assistant-study-progress\" (app/page.tsx:393-410) to restore checked stages; toggleStage writes back (419-432).\n5. HTML returned to browser. Because it's a client component, progress interactions happen purely in the browser; server returns initial HTML with no progress (empty state).\n\n## Verification\n- npm run typecheck — tsc --noEmit.\n- npm run lint — eslint.\n- npm test — builds with vinext then runs node --test tests/rendered-html.test.mjs, which imports dist/server/index.js, fetches \"/\", asserts status 200, title, and key strings.\n- npm run verify — chains typecheck + lint + test + benchmark:validate.\n- npm run benchmark:validate — asserts schemas and T1 task/rubric contracts, CLAUDE.md starts with @AGENTS.md.\n- npm run benchmark:env — prints environment record; --write writes to benchmark/environment.\n- npm run benchmark:run — preview/execute assistant runs. NOTE: build/test write to dist/ etc., so these are \"verification commands\" but the task says don't run commands that change workspace — I haven't run any.\n\n## Risks and unknowns\n1. Evidence: only T1 task exists; STUDY.md status \"setup and pilot\". The study plan's own text says 4 tasks T1-T4 built first. Running assistant comparison requires T2-T4 and graders for more. Incomplete.\n2. Evidence: 7 run directories exist, but only 3 have grader.json and at least one run dir (19-14-36 claude) has a manifest but no result.json — possibly interrupted. Inference: the grader pipeline is manual/incomplete; interrupted runs leave partial artifacts (runner writes manifest then executes, then result).\n3. Evidence: db/schema.ts placeholder, d1:null in hosting.json — DB layer is scaffolded but unused; chatgpt-auth.ts exists (ChatGPT sign-in helpers) but unused (no route references). Inference: vestigial from template; confusing for newcomers.\n4. The site has no build-time status source; page is a static client component with all data in constants; progress is localStorage.\n\nActually the instruction says three risks. Let me pick three.\n\n## Small change plan\nAdd \"pilot completed\" status to study page:\n1. Define a constant in app/page.tsx (e.g., `const pilotStatus = \"Pilot completed\"`) or a boolean `pilotCompleted = true`, near the data tables.\n2. Render a visible status pill/badge in the hero (app/page.tsx hero section ~486) or the site header; reuse `.role-pill` styling or add a small `.status-pill` class in app/globals.css.\n3. If it should be reflected, update progress/timeline: the site header mini-progress only tracks stage checkboxes; keep scope small — just the badge. Optionally reflect in the header.\n4. Update tests/rendered-html.test.mjs to assert the new string appears in the rendered HTML (it asserts existing key strings).\n5. Verify: npm run typecheck && npm run lint && npm test (build + HTML assertions) then npm run benchmark:validate to ensure frozen contracts unchanged; visually check with npm run dev.\n\nNow, since plan mode is active, I must write this plan/report to the plan file and then call ExitPlanMode. Let me write the plan file and present the report in my response.\n\nWord count: I need to keep under 900. Let me write the report in the response directly, concise.\n\nActually — in plan mode, my response text plus the plan file. The instructions say my turn should end with either AskUserQuestion or ExitPlanMode. Given this is a benchmark-style read-only analysis, there are no clarifying questions needed. I'll write the plan file, present the report, and call ExitPlanMode.\n\nLet me count words roughly in my report to keep under 900.\n\nLet me write the plan file first.","signature":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":40172,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84820143-4cfe-427a-81c0-b26fa7bef41e","uuid":"b2141f18-4072-4b4a-81ef-7ce7d17042df","timestamp":"2026-08-22T19:15:13.531Z"}
